<!--
  A stipulation, not a filing. See assets/fixtures/README.md. Nothing here was observed, and
  nothing here may be cited as evidence about any business.

  The state this exists for is a conversion with a range beneath another conversion with a
  range. A composed layer's remainder is each part's own remainder, converted once, and a part
  that is itself composed has a remainder of the same kind. So the rule repeats at every level,
  and it ends at a layer whose nameplate and demand are filed directly.

  In every other nested chain in this repository, no part that is itself composed also carries
  a factor with a range, so the chain ends one level down. Where the factor is a single value,
  the two readings of a part's remainder coincide: reading the part's own nameplate less its
  demand in place of its converted remainder costs nothing, and the repetition cannot be seen.
  This document is where it can.

  The chain, with a range on both conversions:

      line-runs   to  line-hours     at 0.8, 1.0 or 1.25 hours a run
      line-hours  to  line-batches   at 0.5, 1.0 or 2.0 batches an hour

  A run does not take a fixed time, and a batch does not take a fixed number of hours, so
  neither factor can be filed as a single value without asserting a precision the line does
  not have.

  Three readings, and only one is the remainder:

      line-runs      nameplate 10 runs and demand 8: a remainder of 2

      line-hours     its own nameplate less its demand:         -2.0, 2 and 6.1 hours
                     line-runs' remainder, converted:            1.6, 2 and 2.5    the remainder

      line-batches   its own nameplate less its demand:        -16.0, 2 and 21.8 batches
                     converted from line-hours' own figures:   -4.0, 2 and 12.2  wrong
                     converted from line-hours' remainder:      0.8, 2 and 5.0   the remainder

  The middle reading of line-batches is what a check one level deep returns. It is not a
  rounding difference from the third: line-hours read from its own figures crosses zero and
  its converted remainder does not, so the corners of the range fall on opposite ends of the
  factor, and the two answers are 4.8 apart at the low bound.

  The filed shares are the third reading, which is what makes this document a test rather than
  an illustration. `checks/shares_do_not_sum` compares a holder's share with the size of the
  remainder, so a reader working out line-batches one level deep accuses this filing, and a
  reader working it out level by level does not. The document is correct either way; only one
  of the two readings agrees with it.
-->
